<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <RootNamespace>Diaspora.Modules.Conversations</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>Diaspora.Modules.Conversations</AssemblyName>
    <Description>
      The Conversations &amp; Messaging Platform (Phase B13.2). The platform's single owner of a
      conversation, its participants, its messages and everything that happens to them.

      B13 built the Communication Platform and its runtime built the sockets; both stopped
      deliberately short of conversations, because nothing owned one. IRealtimeGroupAuthorizer
      refused every conversation group, ConversationRelayEnabled was false on every deployment, and
      the client's chat screens ran on a mock. This module is the thing that was missing, and it
      arrives entirely through seams the runtime already published.

      What this module owns: the conversation, its participants and their per-participant state
      (archive, mute, pin, read cursor, unread count), every message and its lifecycle, the block
      list, the conversation context type catalogue, conversation authorisation, and the
      orchestration that turns a domain fact into a socket write or a push.

      What it deliberately does not own: any business data. A conversation about a listing holds the
      listing's identifier and a snapshot of how it was described when the conversation opened — it
      holds no price the Marketplace could change, no availability Housing could revoke and no
      status a Job could move. It holds media identifiers, never bytes and never URLs. It holds
      member identifiers, never names — those are resolved through a directory port whose shipped
      implementation returns identifiers, exactly as B13 resolves nothing about people either.

      It owns no transport. Every live write goes through IRealtimeNotifier and every push through
      INotificationDispatcher, both of them B13's ports; this assembly names SignalR nowhere and an
      architecture test says so.
    </Description>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Two module references, and both are seam implementations rather than data access.

    Diaspora.Modules.Notifications, because this module implements four of the Communication
    Runtime's published ports — IRealtimeGroupAuthorizer (the seam that exists precisely so a
    conversations vertical can answer "is this member in this conversation"), IRealtimeNotifier as a
    consumer, INotificationDispatcher for push, and the notification catalogue contributors. The
    reference runs from the vertical to the platform, which is the direction the runtime's own
    documentation describes.

    Diaspora.Modules.Marketplace, because B10 published IMarketplaceConversationProvider with no
    implementation and recorded buyer/seller chat as "future Communication". Fulfilling that seam is
    what the note meant. The adapter lives in Integration/Marketplace and the domain knows nothing
    about it.

    Housing, Jobs, Providers, Community Life and Organizations are integrated through published
    contracts and context identifiers, with no project reference at all.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="MediatR" />
    <PackageReference Include="FluentValidation" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection.Abstractions" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Configuration.Abstractions" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.Abstractions" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Options" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Options.ConfigurationExtensions" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting.Abstractions" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Diaspora.SharedKernel\Diaspora.SharedKernel.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Diaspora.Contracts\Diaspora.Contracts.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Diaspora.Platform\Diaspora.Platform.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Diaspora.Application\Diaspora.Application.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Diaspora.Persistence\Diaspora.Persistence.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Diaspora.Modules.Notifications\Diaspora.Modules.Notifications.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Diaspora.Modules.Marketplace\Diaspora.Modules.Marketplace.csproj" />
    <!--
      Community, for one class: Infrastructure/Community/MessageContentSource.cs.

      B6 owns reporting for every other content type on the platform, and a direct message was the
      one surface with no way to raise a complaint at all. Claiming CommunityContentType.Message
      here — rather than building a second reporting system inside Conversations — keeps one report
      taxonomy, one moderation queue and one set of reasons. The source deliberately resolves only
      for a participant and never exposes a message body; see the class note.
    -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Diaspora.Modules.Community\Diaspora.Modules.Community.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
